 Solomonoff function induction (SFI) is Bayesian prediction of a label $y$ from an input $x$ with a universal prior over random partial functions \citep{claudeSFI2026fundamentals}. It is the supervised-learning counterpart of Solomonoff induction (SI), and like SI it learns every computable regularity of the labels. We study the following proposal, a stronger simplicity prior: penalise the length of a hypothesis more than its likelihood term, so that what is selected is a simple model with noise.\footnote{The proposal first appeared in the author's note \citep{hanni2026grokking} and in an online discussion (on LessWrong), where the absorption objection described below was also raised. So did the suggestion that private randomness at each input blocks this objection, the three-hypothesis calculation below (in both versions of \cref{rem:teacher:hanni}), the question whether its good model is a vertex of the convex hull, and an absolute complexity bound as an alternative. SFI itself first appeared in the author's working notes \citep{hanni2026notes}.} In the form studied here, a hypothesis is a program $\pi$ of length $\ell(\pi)$ with likelihood $P_\pi$, and one minimises $J_\lambda(\pi;D)=\lambda\,\ell(\pi)-\log P_\pi(D)$ with $\lambda>1$, or uses the power prior $2^{-\lambda\ell(\pi)}$ (\cref{def:setup:objectives}), over probabilistic programs that use fresh coins at every input: private randomness.
 
-We give two reasons for such a penalty. The first, and the main one, is to split data into a model and noise: labels drawn by a simple sampler are explained by the sampler and by \emph{fill-ins}, deterministic programs that store its coin flips, and at $\lambda=1$ the two get similar posterior weight on a data set, in SFI and in SI (\cref{thm:split:lambda1,prop:split:si}). Does a steeper prior select the sampler? The second reason is not to learn a teacher's systematic mistakes, as a human learner grasps what a teacher means by a word without learning to predict what the teacher would say. With a $100$-bit simple model and systematic errors on a $2^{-10}$ fraction of the inputs, whose set takes $10{,}000$ bits to specify, the simple model with noise beats a model that learns the errors and a fair coin for a range of $\lambda/n$ (\cref{rem:teacher:hanni}); but three hypotheses are not all hypotheses. Both reasons rest on one geometry, a kink of the hull of the points (length, fit) with the discarded part to its right; noise is used at one input and an error rule at many, so noise needs only a fixed $\lambda>1$ and errors need $\lambda$ proportional to the number $n$ of samples (\cref{tab:discussion:reasons}).
+We give two reasons for such a penalty. The first, and the main one, is to split data into a model and noise: labels drawn by a simple sampler are explained by the sampler and by \emph{fill-ins}, deterministic programs that store its coin flips, and at $\lambda=1$ the two get similar posterior weight on a data set, in SFI and in SI (\cref{thm:split:lambda1,prop:split:si}). Does a steeper prior select the sampler? One motivation is imitation: a learner trained on a person's answers should return a copy of the person, a sampler with its own randomness, rather than the sampler with some of its sampled bits stored, or a different system that predicts the person's answers (\cref{sec:imit}). The second reason is not to learn a teacher's systematic mistakes, as a human learner grasps what a teacher means by a word without learning to predict what the teacher would say. With a $100$-bit simple model and systematic errors on a $2^{-10}$ fraction of the inputs, whose set takes $10{,}000$ bits to specify, the simple model with noise beats a model that learns the errors and a fair coin for a range of $\lambda/n$ (\cref{rem:teacher:hanni}); but three hypotheses are not all hypotheses. Both reasons rest on one geometry, a kink of the hull of the points (length, fit) with the discarded part to its right; noise is used at one input and an error rule at many, so noise needs only a fixed $\lambda>1$ and errors need $\lambda$ proportional to the number $n$ of samples (\cref{tab:discussion:reasons}).
 
 A standard objection, the \emph{absorption objection}, is that under a steeper penalty a hypothesis that is itself a universal mixture is short, penalises complexity inside itself only in the usual way, and so absorbs all structure, noise and errors included. It holds in SI and in SFI with shared randomness (\cref{thm:absorb:absorption,thm:split:shared}); if each input samples its own random bits, no hypothesis is universal (\cref{cor:absorb:nouniversal}).
 
