-\paragraph{Scope and reader's guide.} The positive teacher result is proved only for parities, error sets of product form, uniform inputs, incompressible error rules and flip rates below $0.05596$, or $0.1233$ asymptotically (\cref{rem:teacher:hypotheses,prop:teacher:rates}); for realistic error patterns we have counterexamples but no positive result. Exact recovery holds per query; from labels of a computable function flipped at a computable rate below $\frac12$ it recovers the function ($\mu$-a.e.) and the rate (\cref{cor:recovery:noisy}). With $\lambda=cn$ the learner is a family of batch objectives (\cref{rem:setup:persample}). After related work and the setting (\cref{sec:related,sec:setup}) come absorption, the precondition of both reasons (\cref{sec:absorb}), kinks (\cref{sec:kinks}), the first reason (\cref{sec:recovery}), the shared instance (\cref{sec:parity}), the second reason (\cref{sec:teacher}), where it fails (\cref{sec:limits}) and resource bounds (\cref{sec:computable}), each with an appendix of proofs; \cref{sec:discussion} lists open problems. Notation follows the companion paper.
+\paragraph{Scope and reader's guide.} We do not claim to solve the problems that motivate the two reasons, imitating a person and learning what a teacher means rather than what the teacher says. We offer tools for them: the private-randomness class, the kink geometry, the reuse factor and a per-sample threshold on the rate at which a component buys fit. With them we solve toy cases, for idealised learners that minimise the objective exactly over a hypothesis class, in the main results all programs of a fixed reference machine: exact recovery of a computable sampler from per-query samples with i.i.d.\ inputs (\cref{thm:recovery:main}); the full table of a noisy random parity with a dyadic flip rate below $\frac14$, for large $d$ (\cref{prop:parity:range}); a teacher who flips a random parity on an incompressible error rule of product form, with uniform inputs and flip rates below $0.05596$, or $0.1233$ asymptotically (\cref{thm:teacher:main,rem:teacher:hypotheses,prop:teacher:rates}); and, up to near-copies, a sampler whose coins are an incompressible function of the environment on many more cells than it has bits (\cref{thm:imit:main}); the last two for the population objective, and from samples only asymptotically or with large constants. For realistic error patterns we have counterexamples but no positive result, and the conceptual problems listed in \cref{sec:discussion} remain open. Exact recovery holds per query; from labels of a computable function flipped at a computable rate below $\frac12$ it recovers the function ($\mu$-a.e.) and the rate (\cref{cor:recovery:noisy}). With $\lambda=cn$ the learner is a family of batch objectives (\cref{rem:setup:persample}). After related work and the setting (\cref{sec:related,sec:setup}) come absorption, the precondition of both reasons (\cref{sec:absorb}), kinks (\cref{sec:kinks}), the first reason (\cref{sec:recovery}), the shared instance (\cref{sec:parity}), the second reason (\cref{sec:teacher}), where it fails (\cref{sec:limits}), imitation (\cref{sec:imit}) and resource bounds (\cref{sec:computable}), each with an appendix of proofs; \cref{sec:discussion} lists open problems. Notation follows the companion paper.
